@node	1.1.1, 1.1.2, 1.1, 1.1
@subsection Expressions

One easy way to get started at programming is to examine some typical
fragments of a program in action.  A TypeScript program runs as a script on a
standard host runtime, so this book shows what Scheme's interpreter transcript
showed as runnable listings, each one part of the section's example module,
whose results are asserted by the test file beside it.  In each listing, the
results appear as comment lines beginning with @code{// =>}, so every
transcript in this book is true by construction.  Scheme's interactive
@newterm{read-eval-print loop}---read an expression, evaluate it, print the
result---becomes the listing's run: the expressions are evaluated in order,
and each result is written as a comment where the interpreter would have
printed it.  You type an @newterm{expression} into a source file, the type
checker checks it, and the running program computes the result of its
@newterm{evaluating} that expression.

One kind of primitive expression you might write is a number.  (More precisely,
the expression that you write consists of the numerals that represent the number
in base 10.)  If you present TypeScript with a number

@example typescript
486
// => 486
@end example

@noindent
the running program produces that value, and the listing records
it.@footnote{Throughout this book, when we wish to emphasize the distinction
between the expression written in the source and the result of evaluating it,
we will show the latter as a @code{// =>} comment line.}

Expressions representing numbers may be combined with an expression
representing a primitive operation (such as @code{+} or @code{*}) to form a
compound expression that represents the application of the operation to those
numbers.  For @w{example}:

@example typescript
137 + 349
// => 486

1000 - 334
// => 666

5 * 99
// => 495

10 / 5
// => 2

2.7 + 10
// => 12.7
@end example

@noindent
Expressions such as these, formed by writing an operator between the
expressions that denote its arguments, are the closest TypeScript comes to the
book's @newterm{combinations}.  The vocabulary differs from Lisp's in one
visible way: Lisp writes every combination in @newterm{prefix notation}, with
the operator leftmost and its operands following it, while TypeScript writes
its primitive arithmetic operators in the customary infix position
(@code{137 + 349}).  A function call is prefix-shaped the way a Lisp
combination is---the procedure's name comes first, @code{square(2 + 5)}---but
the operators proper are written between their operands, and each primitive
operator takes a fixed number of arguments, so the original's variadic
operator calls become @code{21 + 35 + 12 + 7}, one operator repeated.
What matters for this chapter is what the two notations share: in both, the
value of a compound expression is obtained by applying the procedure specified
by the @newterm{operator} to the @newterm{arguments} that are the values of
the @newterm{operands}, and in both the notation is unambiguous about which
operator applies to which operands.

A second advantage the book claims for prefix notation is that it extends in a
straightforward way to allow combinations to be @i{nested}, that is, to have
combinations whose elements are themselves combinations.  TypeScript nests the
same way:

@example typescript
3 * 5 + (10 - 6)
// => 19
@end example

@noindent
There is no limit (in principle) to the depth of such nesting and to the
overall complexity of the expressions that the checker will accept and the
running program will evaluate.  It is we humans who get confused by still
relatively simple expressions such as

@example typescript
3 * (2 * 4 + (3 + 5)) + (10 - 7 + 6)
@end example

@noindent
which the checker accepts and the program evaluates to be 57.  We can help
ourselves by writing such an expression in the form

@example typescript
3 * (2 * 4
     + (3 + 5))
   + (10 - 7
      + 6)
@end example

@noindent
following a formatting convention known as @newterm{pretty-printing}, in which
each long expression is written so that the operands are aligned vertically.
The resulting indentations display clearly the structure of the
expression.@footnote{TypeScript ships with formatters that automate this
layout: the edition's own formatter, Biome (@ref{0.1}), prints every expression
in the community's pretty-print convention, so no programmer in this book's
examples formats an expression by hand or argues about indentation.  Editors
likewise highlight the matching parenthesis or brace as you type.}

Even with complex expressions, the running program always operates in the same
basic cycle: it fetches the next statement, evaluates it, and moves on; there
is no moment where the program stops to print anything unless the source asks
it to.  Observe in particular that it is not necessary to explicitly instruct
the program to compute the value of an expression; the value is what evaluation
produces, and the listings record it as a comment.@footnote{Scheme obeys the
convention that every expression has a value, and its read-eval-print loop
prints each one---the source of the quip by Alan Perlis (paraphrasing Oscar
Wilde) that ``Lisp programmers know the value of everything but the cost of
nothing.''  TypeScript gives every expression a value too, but a statement's
value is discarded, which is why the conditional operator @code{? :} exists:
it is the spelling for a case analysis that must produce a value.}

@node	1.1.2, 1.1.3, 1.1.1, 1.1
@subsection Naming and the Environment

A critical aspect of a programming language is the means it provides for using
names to refer to computational objects.  We say that the name identifies a
@newterm{variable} whose @newterm{value} is the object.

In TypeScript, we name things with @code{const}.  Writing

@example typescript
const size = 2;
@end example

@noindent
causes the program to associate the value 2 with the name
@code{size}.@footnote{In this book, we do not show any response to evaluating
definitions: a @code{const} declaration is a statement, not an expression, so
it produces no value to show.} Once the name @code{size} has been associated
with the number 2, we can refer to the value 2 by name:

@example typescript
size
// => 2

5 * size
// => 10
@end example

@noindent
Here are further examples of the use of @code{const}:

@example typescript
const pi = 3.14159;
const radius = 10;

pi * (radius * radius)
// => 314.159

const circumference = 2 * pi * radius;

circumference
// => 62.8318
@end example

@noindent
@code{Const} is our language's simplest means of abstraction, for it allows us
to use simple names to refer to the results of compound operations, such as the
@code{circumference} computed above.  In general, computational objects may
have very complex structures, and it would be extremely inconvenient to have to
remember and repeat their details each time we want to use them.  Indeed,
complex programs are constructed by building, step by step, computational
objects of increasing complexity.  Where the book's Lisp session builds this
way one interaction at a time, a TypeScript program grows the same way in the
source: each new name is written down once, checked by the type checker, and
thereafter available by name, which gives the incremental style a guarantee the
interactive session never had.  (A name bound with @code{const} cannot be
reassigned; the reassignable @code{let} has a section of its own, @ref{0.5}.)

It should be clear that the possibility of associating values with names and
later retrieving them means that some sort of bookkeeping must keep track of
the name-object pairs.  This bookkeeping is called the
@newterm{environment} (more precisely the @newterm{global environment}, since
we will see later that a computation may involve a number of different
environments).  In TypeScript the environment of a module is its set of
scopes: each block and each function body is a scope in which names are bound,
and the module's top-level names are the program's global
ones.@footnote{@ref{Chapter 3} will show that this notion of environment is
essential, both for understanding how programs work and for implementing
interpreters.}

@node	1.1.3, 1.1.4, 1.1.2, 1.1
@subsection Evaluating Combinations

One of our goals in this chapter is to isolate issues about thinking
procedurally.  As a case in point, let us consider that, in evaluating
combinations, the language implementation is itself following a procedure.

@quotation
To evaluate a combination, do the following:

@enumerate 1

@item
Evaluate the subexpressions of the combination.

@item
Apply the procedure that is the value of the leftmost subexpression (the
operator) to the arguments that are the values of the other subexpressions (the
operands).

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@noindent
Even this simple rule illustrates some important points about processes in
general.  First, observe that the first step dictates that in order to
accomplish the evaluation process for a combination we must first perform the
evaluation process on each element of the combination.  Thus, the evaluation
rule is @newterm{recursive} in nature; that is, it includes, as one of its
steps, the need to invoke the rule itself.@footnote{It may seem strange that
the evaluation rule says, as part of the first step, that we should evaluate
the leftmost element of a combination, since at this point that can only be an
operator such as @code{+} or @code{*} representing a built-in primitive
procedure such as addition or multiplication.  We will see later that it is
useful to be able to work with combinations whose operators are themselves
compound expressions.}

Notice how succinctly the idea of recursion can be used to express what, in the
case of a deeply nested combination, would otherwise be viewed as a rather
complicated process.  For example, evaluating

@example typescript
(2 + 4 * 6) * (3 + 5 + 7)
@end example

@noindent
requires that the evaluation rule be applied to four different combinations.
We can obtain a picture of this process by representing the combination in the
form of a tree, as shown in @ref{Figure 1.1}.  Each combination is represented
by a node with branches corresponding to the operator and the operands of the
combination stemming from it.  The terminal nodes (that is, nodes with no
branches stemming from them) represent either operators or numbers.  Viewing
evaluation in terms of the tree, we can imagine that the values of the operands
percolate upward, starting from the terminal nodes and then combining at higher
and higher levels.  In general, we shall see that recursion is a very powerful
technique for dealing with hierarchical, treelike objects.  In fact, the
``percolate values upward'' form of the evaluation rule is an example of a
general kind of process known as @newterm{tree accumulation}.

